%S A325423 1,7,31,37,67,73,97,103,127,157,199,202,229,241,247,262,277,283,307,
%T A325423 313,331,337,346,367,379,382,397,409,427,457,472,487,499,517,547,562,
%U A325423 577,607,619,643,661,667,682,697,727,757,769,787
%N A325423 Numbers k such that sigma(2*k+1) >= sigma(2*k).
%C A325423 The function A(x) enumerating the terms not exceeding x has the property that lim_{x->oo} A(x)/x exists (Adolf Hildebrand, in the Solution to Problem 6555, 1990).
%C A325423 Kobayashi and Trudgian (2020) proved that the asymptotic density of this sequence exists, and that it is in the interval [0.0539171, 0.0549445]. They calculated the number of terms that do not exceed 10^k, for k = 3..9: 60, 551, 5490, 54603, 546879, 546537, and 54665173, and conjectured that the density equals 0.0546... . - _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 06 2026
%H A325423 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A325423/b325423.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..2763 from Giuseppe Melfi)
%H A325423 Mits Kobayashi and Tim Trudgian, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnt.2020.02.007">On integers n for which sigma(2n + 1) >= sigma(2n)</a>, Journal of Number Theory, Vol. 215 (2020), pp. 138-148; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10064">arXiv preprint</a>, arXiv:1904.10064 [math.NT], 2019.
%H A325423 Moshe Laub, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2323430">Probelm 6555</a>, Advanced Problems, The American Mathematical Monthly, Vol. 94, No. 8 (1987), p. 800; <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2324532">Odd Integers with Relatively Large Divisor Sum</a>, Solution to Problem 6555, by L. E. Mattics, ibid., Vol. 97, No. 4 (1990), pp. 351-353.
%F A325423 a(n) ~ c*n with 18.2 < c < 18.6 (claimed by Kobayashi and Trudgian).
%e A325423 7 is in the sequence because sigma(14) = 1+2+7+14 = 24 <= sigma(15) = 1+3+5+15 = 24.
%e A325423 31 is in the sequence because sigma(62) = 1+2+31+62 = 96 <= sigma(63) = 1+3+7+9+21+63 = 104.
%t A325423 Position[Partition[DivisorSigma[1,Range[2,1601]],2],_?(#[[2]] >= #[[1]]&),1,Heads->False]//Flatten (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Jan 10 2022 *)
%o A325423 (PARI) isok(n) = sigma(2*n+1) >= sigma(2*n); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Sep 09 2019
